1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,541
In 2018,

2
00:00:00,541 --> 00:00:05,073
investor and AngelList founder Naval Ravikant published a blueprint

3
00:00:05,073 --> 00:00:08,726
that Eric Jorgenson later curated into The Almanack of

4
00:00:08,726 --> 00:00:09,741
Naval Ravikant.

5
00:00:09,741 --> 00:00:12,379
Its first pillar is Specific Knowledge:

6
00:00:12,379 --> 00:00:16,438
the skills you cannot be taught in a standardized classroom.

7
00:00:16,438 --> 00:00:19,414
Naval warns that if society can train you by

8
00:00:19,414 --> 00:00:20,902
following a checklist,

9
00:00:20,902 --> 00:00:25,367
society can eventually replace you with cheaper labor or software.

10
00:00:25,367 --> 00:00:29,425
He describes specific knowledge as something that feels like

11
00:00:29,425 --> 00:00:32,266
play to you but looks like work to others.

12
00:00:32,266 --> 00:00:35,987
Ask yourself what felt like effortless curiosity to you

13
00:00:35,987 --> 00:00:39,234
ten years ago that still feels like tedious work

14
00:00:39,234 --> 00:00:41,060
to people around you today.

15
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:44,101
Once you identify your specific knowledge,

16
00:00:44,101 --> 00:00:46,926
Model Two asks how far a single hour of

17
00:00:46,926 --> 00:00:48,736
your judgment can travel.

18
00:00:48,736 --> 00:00:50,546
Archimedes famously said,

19
00:00:50,546 --> 00:00:53,371
give me a lever long enough and a place

20
00:00:53,371 --> 00:00:54,022
to stand,

21
00:00:54,022 --> 00:00:55,905
and I will move the earth.

22
00:00:55,905 --> 00:00:56,919
For centuries,

23
00:00:56,919 --> 00:01:00,829
business relied on two forms of permissioned leverage:

24
00:01:00,829 --> 00:01:01,264
labor,

25
00:01:01,264 --> 00:01:03,509
meaning people working for you,

26
00:01:03,509 --> 00:01:04,378
and capital,

27
00:01:04,378 --> 00:01:06,623
meaning money under management.

28
00:01:06,623 --> 00:01:09,447
Both require someone else's permission.

29
00:01:09,447 --> 00:01:11,547
Naval points to a newer kind:

30
00:01:11,547 --> 00:01:14,588
products that cost almost nothing to copy,

31
00:01:14,588 --> 00:01:16,181
mainly code and media.

32
00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:19,875
You do not need a venture capitalist's signature to

33
00:01:19,875 --> 00:01:21,468
write a Python script,

34
00:01:21,468 --> 00:01:24,002
publish an educational video essay,

35
00:01:24,002 --> 00:01:25,957
or ship a digital template.

36
00:01:25,957 --> 00:01:29,723
Recording a video for ten thousand viewers costs the

37
00:01:29,723 --> 00:01:32,330
same effort as recording it for ten.

38
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:35,592
Model Three is one of the sharpest wake-up calls

39
00:01:35,592 --> 00:01:37,427
in modern personal finance:

40
00:01:37,427 --> 00:01:40,690
you are not going to gain true financial freedom

41
00:01:40,690 --> 00:01:42,593
renting out your time alone.

42
00:01:42,593 --> 00:01:45,176
Even high-status salaried professions,

43
00:01:45,176 --> 00:01:46,127
like surgeons,

44
00:01:46,127 --> 00:01:47,487
corporate attorneys,

45
00:01:47,487 --> 00:01:49,050
and senior consultants,

46
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:51,021
face a strict biological cap.

47
00:01:51,021 --> 00:01:52,040
When you sleep,

48
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:53,128
take a vacation,

49
00:01:53,128 --> 00:01:53,943
or burn out,

50
00:01:53,943 --> 00:01:55,371
metered income stops.

51
00:01:55,371 --> 00:01:57,750
Real wealth requires owning equity:

52
00:01:57,750 --> 00:01:59,245
a piece of a business,

53
00:01:59,245 --> 00:02:00,740
intellectual property,

54
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:01,352
software,

55
00:02:01,352 --> 00:02:02,439
a media catalog,

56
00:02:02,439 --> 00:02:06,857
or diversified productive assets that earn while you are offline.

57
00:02:06,857 --> 00:02:09,780
Keep your salary as a launchpad to pay your

58
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:11,207
bills without stress,

59
00:02:11,207 --> 00:02:15,489
then systematically channel your surplus hours and savings into

60
00:02:15,489 --> 00:02:17,120
assets you actually own.

61
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:20,193
Model Four flips hustle culture upside down.

62
00:02:20,193 --> 00:02:21,381
Without leverage,

63
00:02:21,381 --> 00:02:25,083
working eighty hours a week produces roughly twice as

64
00:02:25,083 --> 00:02:26,620
much as working forty.

65
00:02:26,620 --> 00:02:27,598
With leverage,

66
00:02:27,598 --> 00:02:31,719
exhausted hours can destroy value because tired brains make

67
00:02:31,719 --> 00:02:33,325
sloppy strategic calls.

68
00:02:33,325 --> 00:02:35,491
Imagine steering a supertanker:

69
00:02:35,491 --> 00:02:39,542
a captain who chooses the right course ninety-five percent

70
00:02:39,542 --> 00:02:42,196
of the time is worth far more than one

71
00:02:42,196 --> 00:02:45,340
who is right eighty-five percent of the time,

72
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:48,623
even if the second captain works twice as hard.

73
00:02:48,623 --> 00:02:51,556
Judgment is the skill leverage multiplies,

74
00:02:51,556 --> 00:02:54,770
so protect the time you use to read and think.

75
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:58,402
Treat your calendar like a fortress so your judgment

76
00:02:58,402 --> 00:02:59,240
stays sharp.

77
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:02,981
Model Five explains why impatience ruins smart builders.

78
00:03:02,981 --> 00:03:05,118
Every meaningful reward in life,

79
00:03:05,118 --> 00:03:06,187
whether capital,

80
00:03:06,187 --> 00:03:07,122
skill mastery,

81
00:03:07,122 --> 00:03:08,124
audience trust,

82
00:03:08,124 --> 00:03:09,193
or partnerships,

83
00:03:09,193 --> 00:03:11,130
comes from compound interest.

84
00:03:11,130 --> 00:03:14,737
Yet human beings constantly reset the exponent back to

85
00:03:14,737 --> 00:03:17,676
day one by jumping to shiny new trends every

86
00:03:17,676 --> 00:03:21,282
ninety days or cutting ethical corners for quick wins.

87
00:03:21,282 --> 00:03:25,424
When you work with high-integrity collaborators over a decade,

88
00:03:25,424 --> 00:03:29,565
you stop wasting energy on legal friction and office politics.

89
00:03:29,565 --> 00:03:31,970
Put accountability behind your work,

90
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:34,174
deliver exactly what you promise,

91
00:03:34,174 --> 00:03:37,380
and stay in one compounding lane long enough for

92
00:03:37,380 --> 00:03:39,050
the curve to bend upward.

93
00:03:39,050 --> 00:03:41,520
How do you actually sharpen judgment?

94
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,791
Model Six echoes Nobel physicist Richard Feynman:

95
00:03:44,791 --> 00:03:48,196
real thinkers never hide behind complicated jargon.

96
00:03:48,196 --> 00:03:53,003
Naval argues it is far better to understand foundational microeconomics,

97
00:03:53,003 --> 00:03:54,204
basic probability,

98
00:03:54,204 --> 00:03:55,339
human psychology,

99
00:03:55,339 --> 00:03:59,011
and clear persuasive writing at a rock-solid level than

100
00:03:59,011 --> 00:04:02,149
to memorize advanced buzzwords on shaky ground.

101
00:04:02,149 --> 00:04:06,355
Read long-tested primary books rather than daily outrage feeds.

102
00:04:06,355 --> 00:04:09,359
If you cannot explain why a business model or

103
00:04:09,359 --> 00:04:12,163
habit system works on the back of a napkin

104
00:04:12,163 --> 00:04:13,298
in plain English,

105
00:04:13,298 --> 00:04:16,302
you do not understand it deeply enough to bet

106
00:04:16,302 --> 00:04:17,370
your time on it.

107
00:04:17,370 --> 00:04:17,962
Finally,

108
00:04:17,962 --> 00:04:21,588
Model Seven turns philosophy into daily behavior.

109
00:04:21,588 --> 00:04:24,031
Long before Naval had real money,

110
00:04:24,031 --> 00:04:27,583
he told himself his time was worth five thousand

111
00:04:27,583 --> 00:04:28,767
dollars an hour.

112
00:04:28,767 --> 00:04:31,875
If outsourcing a task cost less than that,

113
00:04:31,875 --> 00:04:33,133
he outsourced it.

114
00:04:33,133 --> 00:04:36,168
If fixing a problem saved less than that,

115
00:04:36,168 --> 00:04:37,204
he ignored it.

116
00:04:37,204 --> 00:04:38,166
Looking back,

117
00:04:38,166 --> 00:04:41,126
he says his real rate then was closer to

118
00:04:41,126 --> 00:04:41,940
a thousand.

119
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:45,271
You can start at fifty or one hundred dollars

120
00:04:45,271 --> 00:04:46,307
an hour today.

121
00:04:46,307 --> 00:04:47,195
This Sunday,

122
00:04:47,195 --> 00:04:48,897
run a three-step audit:

123
00:04:48,897 --> 00:04:52,597
write down the three skills you combine naturally,

124
00:04:52,597 --> 00:04:55,335
eliminate one low-value weekly chore,

125
00:04:55,335 --> 00:04:59,258
and invest five protected hours shipping one piece of

126
00:04:59,258 --> 00:05:01,700
self-published media or software.
